Mūla-text

The mūla-text (in blue) is Vallabha's, verses not given by all
commentators are written in a lighter blue, variant readings are given in
a negative critical apparatus under the verse, in the mūla the
corresponding words are marked in a deeper blue.

Brackets in the critical apparatus refer to variant readings mentioned by
a commentator, question marks signify that the reading is only given by
the editor and cannot be confirmed by the commentary. This is the case
with Dakṣiṇāvartanātha, as he does not comment on all the words of the
mūla.

Variant numbers of verses are given on the right of the Mūla.

The number at the end of the commentaries refers to the count of the
corresponding commentaries, the blue one to the number assigned to the
verse by Vallabha.

If a verse is not commented upon by Vallabha or declared spurious by him
it is given the number -a etc. of the preceding one. For example the
verses II.12 and II.13 of Mallinātha are numbered 70a and 70b, because
II.11 of Mallinātha corresponds to 70 of Vallabhadeva.

As Dakṣiṇāvartanātha and Mallinātha divide into Purva- and
Uttaramegha a new count is begun with the Uttaramegha, while the count for
Vallabha and Sumativijaya continues.

I have typed Sumativijaya's commentary only for the Purvamegha, because I
got so bored with his innumerable kimbhūtas that I couldn't stand it any
longer. May someone whose ability to endure boredom excels mine by far
finish it some day.

Colours and the like

Bold types are used for words taken from the Mūla, direct glosses
(meaning glosses that are given without the repetition of the Mūla) are
underlined.

Different readings mentioned by the commentators are shown in dark blue,
the reading the commentator holds to be the right one in the same colour
in bold types.

ĕlements that just serve to make the text more explicit and not really
necessary for its understanding are marked grey. These include:

- kathambhūtas and the like,

- the expanded analysis of compounds, for example yasya sa tathoktas

- questions and  sentences that structure the text, like kathamiti
āha, .... ityāśaṅkyāha etc.

- words that serve to explain the syntactical relation like kṛtvā
denoting the instrumental  and so on.

- and last not least the Avataranas, that are not given before the verse
but at the beginning of the commentary, because I think that's the place
where they belong to.

ĕxpressions helping to explain the text are marked brown, namely iti
arthas, iti bhāvas, iti yāvat, ityanusandheyam, iti abhiprāyas and
ityalam vistareṇa/atiprasaṅgena.

Grammatical explanations are given in a dark  green,

Reference to other commentors in light green.

Names of lexicographers are marked with a dotted line.

̈uotations are written in italics. Wherever I traced them down, their
sources are put in these very nice {brackets}. p in the brackets denotes
Pāṇini's Aṣṭadhyāyi, the other abbreviation should be easily
understood. When no abbreviation is given the reference is to the work
mentioned in the text, when no work is mentioned in the text the reference
is to the Meghadūta.

Square brackets [...] are used to show letters or words I have added; when
they have a question mark [....?] they offer a reading I would propose.

Round brackets (....) denote words that are obviously added, when the name
of a commentator is given in them they hint to an identical explanation or
quotation by the commentator mentioned.  As it is not done rather
unsystematically many more paralells can still be found.

̈uestions

At the end of some commentaries I have put questions regarding words or
sentences, that, in my opinion, are problematic. But this has not been
done systematically and many more question marks could be placed, as all
the texts exhibit many incongruencies and contradictions.

And now

link your mind with the mind of Kalidāsa and his commentators and have a
good time with the

Meghadūta

